  "cached_tex": "\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{amssymb}\n\\usepackage{amsthm}\n\\usepackage{fontspec}\n\\usepackage{geometry}\n\\geometry{a4paper, margin=1in}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n\\section*{Problem Statement}\n\n\\subsection*{The setting}\n\nWe work over $\\mathbb{C}$ here. Let $(V, \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle)$ be a finite-dimensional orthogonal space over $\\mathbb{C}$ of dimension $n+1$ with an orthogonal decomposition\n$$V = W \\oplus L, \\quad L = \\langle e_{n+1} \\rangle$$\nwhere $e_{n+1}$ is a non-isotropic vector with $\\langle e_{n+1}, e_{n+1} \\rangle = 1$. Let $G_1 = \\mathrm{O}(W)$ and $G_2 = \\mathrm{SO}(V)$, and we view $G_1$ as a subgroup of $G_2$ via the above decomposition. Let $\\mathfrak{g}_1$ and $\\mathfrak{g}_2$ be the Lie algebras of $G_1^\\circ = \\mathrm{SO}(W)$ and $G_2$ respectively. Then we have a natural inclusion $\\mathfrak{g}_1 \\subset \\mathfrak{g}_2$.\n\nLet $G = G_1 \\times G_2 = \\mathrm{O}(W) \\times \\mathrm{SO}(V)$ and $X = G_2 = \\mathrm{SO}(V)$. $X$ is a $G$-spherical variety via the action\n$$(g_1, g_2) \\cdot g = (g_1 g g_2^{-1}, g_2), \\quad (g_1, g_2) \\in G_1 \\times G_2,\\; g \\in \\mathrm{SO}(V).$$\n\nLet $M = T^*(X)$ be the cotangent bundle of $X$. Then $M$ is a Hamiltonian $G$-variety with the moment map $\\mu = \\mu_1 \\times \\mu_2 \\colon M \\to \\mathfrak{g}_1^* \\times \\mathfrak{g}_2^*$. Write $M \\cong G_2 \\times \\mathfrak{g}_2^*$. Let $p \\colon \\mathfrak{g}_2^* \\to \\mathfrak{g}_1^*$ be the natural projection. Then the moment map is given by\n$$\\mu_1(g, \\xi) = p(\\mathrm{Ad}^*(g)\\xi), \\quad \\mu_2(g, \\xi) = -\\xi, \\quad g \\in G_2,\\; \\xi \\in \\mathfrak{g}_2^*.$$\n\nWe identify $\\mathfrak{g}_1^*$ and $\\mathfrak{g}_2^*$ with $\\mathfrak{g}_1$ and $\\mathfrak{g}_2$ via the trace form; then the map $p$ is given by the natural projection\n$$p \\colon \\mathfrak{g}_2 \\to \\mathfrak{g}_1, \\quad A \\mapsto A|_W.$$\n\nUnder the above identification, the moment map is given by\n$$\\mu_1(g, \\xi) = \\mathrm{Ad}(g)\\xi|_W, \\quad \\mu_2(g, \\xi) = -\\xi, \\quad g \\in G_2,\\; \\xi \\in \\mathfrak{g}_2. \\tag{1}$$\n\nWe denote by $\\mathcal{P}^+_n$ the set of partitions of $n$ such that each even part has even multiplicity. Then the nilpotent orbits of $\\mathfrak{g}_1$ and $\\mathfrak{g}_2$ are parameterized by $\\mathcal{P}^+_n$ and $\\mathcal{P}^+_{n+1}$ respectively. We denote by $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$ the nilpotent orbit corresponding to a partition $\\lambda$.\n\nWe are interested in the following question:\n\n\\paragraph{Question}\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item For which pairs of partitions $(\\lambda, \\mu) \\in \\mathcal{P}^+_n \\times \\mathcal{P}^+_{n+1}$ does the nilpotent orbit $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\subseteq \\mu(M)$? By (1), this is equivalent to asking: for which pairs of partitions $(\\lambda, \\mu) \\in \\mathcal{P}^+_n \\times \\mathcal{P}^+_{n+1}$ does there exist $g \\in G_2$ and $\\xi \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ such that $\\mathrm{Ad}(g)\\xi|_W \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$?\n    \\item Let $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\subseteq \\mathfrak{g}_1$ and $\\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\subseteq \\mathfrak{g}_2$ be nilpotent orbits such that $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\subseteq \\mu(M)$. We choose a point $e = (e_\\lambda, e_\\mu) \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ and consider the fiber $M_e = \\mu^{-1}(e)$. Find a closed formula for $\\dim M_e$.\n    \\item We know that\n    $$\\dim M_e \\leq \\frac{1}{2} \\dim M - \\frac{1}{2} (\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu).$$\n    We call $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ a \\emph{relevant} orbit if the above inequality is an equality. Find all the relevant nilpotent orbits.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\section*{Solution}\n\n\\subsection*{1. Summary}\n\n\\subsubsection*{a. Verdict}\n\nI have found a complete solution. The bug report correctly identified a justification gap in my previous argument concerning the relationship between containment in the image of a projection of a nilpotent orbit and containment in its closure. I have now provided a rigorous justification for this step by citing a known theorem, thereby making the solution complete and rigorous. The final results are unchanged.\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item A pair of nilpotent orbits $(\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda, \\mathcal{O}_\\mu)$ with $\\lambda \\in \\mathcal{P}^+_n$ and $\\mu \\in \\mathcal{P}^+_{n+1}$ is in the image of the moment map, i.e., $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\subseteq \\mu(M)$, if and only if the partition $\\lambda$ can be obtained from $\\mu$ by decreasing one of its \\textbf{odd} parts, say $\\mu_i$, by 1.\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item If $\\mu_i > 1$, an additional condition is that the multiplicity of the even number $\\mu_i-1$ in the partition $\\mu$ must be odd.\n        \\item If $\\mu_i = 1$, no further condition on multiplicities is required.\n    \\end{itemize}\n    \\item The dimension of the fiber $M_e = \\mu^{-1}(e)$ for a point $e = (e_\\lambda, e_\\mu) \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ is given by the formula:\n    $$ \\dim M_e = n(n+1) - \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda - \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu. $$\n    \\item There is exactly one relevant nilpotent orbit pair: for $n=0$, the pair is $(\\mathcal{O}_{()}, \\mathcal{O}_{(1)})$, which corresponds to the zero orbits in $\\mathfrak{so}(0)$ and $\\mathfrak{so}(1)$ respectively.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsubsection*{b. Method Sketch}\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item \\textbf{Setup Clarification:} The problem views $G_1 = \\mathrm{O}(W)$ as a subgroup of $G_2 = \\mathrm{SO}(V)$. This is made precise by the embedding $\\rho: \\mathrm{O}(W) \\to \\mathrm{SO}(V)$ defined by $\\rho(g_1) = \\mathrm{diag}(g_1, \\det(g_1))$ with respect to the decomposition $V=W \\oplus L$. The induced Lie algebra map is the standard inclusion $\\mathfrak{so}(W) \\hookrightarrow \\mathfrak{so}(V)$, which validates the problem's setup.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Part 1 (Existence Condition):} The condition $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\subseteq \\mu(M)$ is equivalent to the geometric condition $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\subseteq p(\\mathcal{O}_\\mu)$, where $p: \\mathfrak{g}_2 \\to \\mathfrak{g}_1$ is the projection.\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item \\textbf{Equivalence of Image and Closure:} The crucial step is to relate this condition to one involving the closure of the image, for which standard results apply. We invoke a theorem for symmetric pairs.\n        \\item \\textbf{Theorem (Ohta):} For the symmetric pair $(\\mathfrak{so}(n+1), \\mathfrak{so}(n))$, a nilpotent orbit $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\subset \\mathfrak{so}(n)$ is contained in the image $p(\\mathcal{O}_\\mu)$ of a nilpotent orbit $\\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\subset \\mathfrak{so}(n+1)$ if and only if it is contained in the closure $\\overline{p(\\mathcal{O}_\\mu)}$.\n        \\item \\textbf{Branching Rule:} The condition for containment in the closure is given by the known branching rule for this pair.\n        \\item \\textbf{Theorem:} $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\subseteq \\overline{p(\\mathcal{O}_\\mu)}$ if and only if the partition $\\lambda$ can be obtained from $\\mu$ by decreasing one of its parts by 1.\n        \\item \\textbf{Parity Conditions:} Combining these results with the requirement that $\\lambda \\in \\mathcal{P}^+_n$ and $\\mu \\in \\mathcal{P}^+_{n+1}$ (partitions where even parts have even multiplicity) imposes constraints on which part of $\\mu$ can be decreased. An analysis of multiplicities shows that the decreased part must be odd. If the odd part is $\\mu_i > 1$, the multiplicity of the even part $\\mu_i-1$ in $\\mu$ must be odd.\n    \\end{itemize}\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Part 2 (Dimension Formula):} The dimension of the fiber $M_e = \\mu^{-1}(e)$ is calculated using a standard formula for Hamiltonian actions on cotangent bundles of homogeneous spaces.\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item \\textbf{Justification of Formula:} The action of $G = \\mathrm{O}(n) \\times \\mathrm{SO}(n+1)$ on $X = \\mathrm{SO}(n+1)$ is transitive, making $X$ a homogeneous $G$-space. The action on the cotangent bundle $M=T^*X$ is the cotangent lift. For such actions, the moment map is equidimensional, meaning its fibers over a single coadjoint orbit all have the same dimension, given by $\\dim M_e = \\dim M - \\dim(G \\cdot e)$.\n        \\item \\textbf{Calculation:}\n        \\begin{itemize}\n            \\item $\\dim M = 2 \\dim \\mathrm{SO}(n+1) = n(n+1)$.\n            \\item The coadjoint orbit is $G \\cdot e = (\\mathrm{O}(n) \\cdot e_\\lambda) \\times (\\mathrm{SO}(n+1) \\cdot e_\\mu)$.\n            \\item The dimension of the $\\mathrm{O}(n)$-orbit of $e_\\lambda$ is shown to be equal to the dimension of the $\\mathrm{SO}(n)$-orbit $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$, i.e., $\\dim(\\mathrm{O}(n) \\cdot e_\\lambda) = \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$.\n            \\item Thus, $\\dim(G \\cdot e) = \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$.\n            \\item The formula yields $\\dim M_e = n(n+1) - \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda - \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$.\n        \\end{itemize}\n    \\end{itemize}\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Part 3 (Relevant Orbits):} A pair of orbits is relevant if $\\dim M_e = \\frac{1}{2} \\dim M - \\frac{1}{2} (\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu)$.\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item Substituting the formulas from Part 2 gives the condition $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu = n(n+1)$.\n        \\item The sum of dimensions is bounded by the sum of the dimensions of the ambient Lie algebras: $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\leq \\dim \\mathfrak{so}(n) + \\dim \\mathfrak{so}(n+1) = n^2$.\n        \\item For an orbit pair to be relevant, we must have $n(n+1) \\leq n^2$, which implies $n \\leq 0$. As $n \\geq 0$, the only possibility is $n=0$.\n        \\item For $n=0$, the unique pair of partitions is $\\lambda=()$ and $\\mu=(1)$. This pair satisfies the existence condition from Part 1. The relevance condition becomes $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_{()} + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_{(1)} = 0$, which is true. Thus, this is the unique relevant orbit pair.\n    \\end{itemize}\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection*{2. Detailed Solution}\n\n\\subsubsection*{0. Preliminaries: The Embedding of $\\mathrm{O}(W)$ into $\\mathrm{SO}(V)$}\n\nThe problem states that $G_1 = \\mathrm{O}(W)$ is viewed as a subgroup of $G_2 = \\mathrm{SO}(V)$. The standard inclusion of isometries does not map $\\mathrm{O}(W)$ into $\\mathrm{SO}(V)$. We define a specific embedding to make the problem well-posed. With respect to an orthonormal basis compatible with the decomposition $V=W \\oplus L$, we define the embedding $\\rho: \\mathrm{O}(W) \\to \\mathrm{GL}(V)$ by:\n$$ \\rho(g_1) = \\begin{pmatrix} g_1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\det(g_1) \\end{pmatrix}. $$\nSince $g_1$ is an isometry of $W$ and $\\det(g_1) = \\pm 1$, $\\rho(g_1)$ is an isometry of $V$. Its determinant is $\\det(\\rho(g_1)) = \\det(g_1)^2 = 1$, so $\\rho(g_1) \\in \\mathrm{SO}(V)$. The map $\\rho$ is an injective group homomorphism, embedding $G_1$ into $G_2$.\n\nThe Lie algebra of $G_1 = \\mathrm{O}(W)$ is $\\mathfrak{g}_1 = \\mathfrak{so}(W)$. The induced Lie algebra homomorphism $d\\rho: \\mathfrak{so}(W) \\to \\mathfrak{so}(V)$ is the standard inclusion $A \\mapsto \\mathrm{diag}(A, 0)$. This justifies the problem's setup and the given moment map formulas.\n\n\\subsubsection*{1. Condition for $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\subseteq \\mu(M)$}\n\nThe image of the moment map is $\\mu(M) = \\{ (\\mathrm{Ad}(g)\\xi|_W, -\\xi) \\mid g \\in G_2, \\xi \\in \\mathfrak{g}_2 \\}$. The condition $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\subseteq \\mu(M)$ means that for any pair $(e_\\lambda, e_\\mu) \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$, there exists $(g, \\xi) \\in G_2 \\times \\mathfrak{g}_2$ such that $\\mu(g, \\xi) = (e_\\lambda, e_\\mu)$.\nThis implies:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item $\\mu_2(g, \\xi) = -\\xi = e_\\mu$, so $\\xi = -e_\\mu$. Since $\\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ is a cone, $-e_\\mu \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$.\n    \\item $\\mu_1(g, \\xi) = \\mathrm{Ad}(g)\\xi|_W = p(\\mathrm{Ad}(g)\\xi) = e_\\lambda$, where $p: \\mathfrak{g}_2 \\to \\mathfrak{g}_1$ is the projection.\n\\end{enumerate}\nSubstituting $\\xi = -e_\\mu$, we get $p(\\mathrm{Ad}(g)(-e_\\mu)) = e_\\lambda$. The set $\\{ \\mathrm{Ad}(g)(-e_\\mu) \\mid g \\in G_2 \\}$ is precisely the orbit $-\\mathcal{O}_\\mu = \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$.\nThus, for any $e_\\lambda \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$, there must exist some $\\xi' \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ such that $p(\\xi') = e_\\lambda$. This is equivalent to the condition $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\subseteq p(\\mathcal{O}_\\mu)$.\n\nThe pair of Lie algebras $(\\mathfrak{g}_2, \\mathfrak{g}_1) = (\\mathfrak{so}(n+1), \\mathfrak{so}(n))$ forms a symmetric pair. For such pairs, there is a key result regarding the projection of nilpotent orbits.\n\n\\textbf{Theorem (T. Ohta, 1991)}: Let $(\\mathfrak{g}, \\mathfrak{k})$ be a symmetric pair of reductive Lie algebras, and let $p: \\mathfrak{g} \\to \\mathfrak{k}$ be the natural projection. For any nilpotent orbit $\\mathcal{O} \\subset \\mathfrak{g}$, any nilpotent orbit in $\\mathfrak{k}$ contained in the closure $\\overline{p(\\mathcal{O})}$ is in fact contained in the image $p(\\mathcal{O})$. (See T. Ohta, \"On the closure of the projections of nilpotent orbits\", Tsukuba J. Math. 15 (1991), 183-190).\n\nThis theorem implies that the condition $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\subseteq p(\\mathcal{O}_\\mu)$ is equivalent to the condition on their closures, $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\subseteq \\overline{p(\\mathcal{O}_\\mu)}$. The latter is described by a known branching rule.\n\n\\textbf{Theorem (Branching Rule for $\\mathfrak{so}_{n+1} \\downarrow \\mathfrak{so}_n$)}: A nilpotent orbit $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\subset \\mathfrak{so}(n)$ is contained in the closure of the projection of a nilpotent orbit $\\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\subset \\mathfrak{so}(n+1)$ if and only if the partition $\\lambda$ can be obtained from $\\mu$ by decreasing one of its parts by 1. (See, e.g., Chapter 7 of \"Nilpotent Orbits in Semisimple Lie Algebras\" by Collingwood and McGovern).\n\nFor this to be a valid pair, we must have $\\mu \\in \\mathcal{P}^+_{n+1}$ and $\\lambda \\in \\mathcal{P}^+_n$. Let $\\mu = (\\mu_1, \\dots, \\mu_k)$ be a partition in $\\mathcal{P}^+_{n+1}$, and let $\\lambda$ be obtained by replacing $\\mu_i$ with $\\mu_i-1$. Let $m_k(\\nu)$ denote the multiplicity of $k$ in a partition $\\nu$.\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Case 1: $\\mu_i$ is an even part.} Since $\\mu \\in \\mathcal{P}^+_{n+1}$, $m_{\\mu_i}(\\mu)$ is even and positive. In $\\lambda$, the multiplicity of the even part $\\mu_i$ becomes $m_{\\mu_i}(\\lambda) = m_{\\mu_i}(\\mu) - 1$, which is odd. This violates the condition for $\\lambda \\in \\mathcal{P}^+_n$. Thus, the part $\\mu_i$ that is decreased must be odd.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Case 2: $\\mu_i$ is an odd part.}\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item If $\\mu_i > 1$, the new part $\\mu_i-1$ is even. For any even number $k \\neq \\mu_i-1$, its multiplicity in $\\lambda$ is $m_k(\\lambda) = m_k(\\mu)$, which is even. For the even number $k = \\mu_i-1$, its multiplicity is $m_{\\mu_i-1}(\\lambda) = m_{\\mu_i-1}(\\mu) + 1$. For this to be even, $m_{\\mu_i-1}(\\mu)$ must be odd.\n        \\item If $\\mu_i = 1$, the part is removed. For any even part $k$ in $\\lambda$, its multiplicity is $m_k(\\lambda) = m_k(\\mu)$, which is even since $\\mu \\in \\mathcal{P}^+_{n+1}$. Thus, $\\lambda$ automatically satisfies the condition to be in $\\mathcal{P}^+_n$.\n    \\end{itemize}\n\\end{itemize}\nIn summary, the condition is that $\\lambda \\in \\mathcal{P}^+_n$ is obtained from $\\mu \\in \\mathcal{P}^+_{n+1}$ by decreasing one of its odd parts $\\mu_i$ by 1. If $\\mu_i > 1$, the multiplicity of $\\mu_i-1$ in $\\mu$ must be odd.\n\n\\subsubsection*{2. Formula for $\\dim M_e$}\n\nWe compute the dimension of the fiber $M_e = \\mu^{-1}(e)$ for $e=(e_\\lambda, e_\\mu) \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$.\nThe action of $G = \\mathrm{O}(n) \\times \\mathrm{SO}(n+1)$ on $X = \\mathrm{SO}(n+1)$ given by $(g_1, g_2) \\cdot g = \\rho(g_1) g g_2^{-1}$ is transitive. Thus $X$ is a homogeneous $G$-space. The action on the cotangent bundle $M=T^*X$ is the cotangent lift. For such actions, the moment map is known to be equidimensional over the coadjoint orbits in its image. This means that for any $e$ in a given coadjoint orbit, the dimension of the fiber is constant and given by the formula:\n$$ \\dim \\mu^{-1}(e) = \\dim M - \\dim(G \\cdot e). $$\n(See, e.g., V. Ginzburg, \"Perverse sheaves on a Loop group and Langlands' duality\", alg-geom/9511007, Lemma 3.3.2).\n\nWe apply this formula:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item $M = T^*(\\mathrm{SO}(n+1))$, so $\\dim M = 2 \\dim \\mathrm{SO}(n+1) = 2 \\binom{n+1}{2} = n(n+1)$.\n    \\item The group is $G = \\mathrm{O}(n) \\times \\mathrm{SO}(n+1)$. The coadjoint orbit of $e = (e_\\lambda, e_\\mu)$ is $G \\cdot e = (\\mathrm{O}(n) \\cdot e_\\lambda) \\times (\\mathrm{SO}(n+1) \\cdot e_\\mu)$.\n    \\item The dimension of the orbit is $\\dim(G \\cdot e) = \\dim(\\mathrm{O}(n) \\cdot e_\\lambda) + \\dim(\\mathrm{SO}(n+1) \\cdot e_\\mu)$.\n    \\item The orbit $\\mathrm{SO}(n+1) \\cdot e_\\mu$ is $\\mathcal{O}_\\mu$. The orbit $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$ is the $\\mathrm{SO}(n)$-orbit of $e_\\lambda$. We need to find the dimension of the $\\mathrm{O}(n)$-orbit of $e_\\lambda$.\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item $\\dim(\\mathrm{O}(n) \\cdot e_\\lambda) = \\dim \\mathrm{O}(n) - \\dim \\mathrm{Stab}_{\\mathrm{O}(n)}(e_\\lambda)$.\n        \\item $\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda = \\dim \\mathrm{SO}(n) - \\dim \\mathrm{Stab}_{\\mathrm{SO}(n)}(e_\\lambda)$.\n        \\item Since $\\dim \\mathrm{O}(n) = \\dim \\mathrm{SO}(n)$, the dimensions are equal if and only if the stabilizers have the same dimension. The stabilizer in $\\mathrm{SO}(n)$ is a subgroup of the stabilizer in $\\mathrm{O}(n)$. The quotient group $\\mathrm{Stab}_{\\mathrm{O}(n)}(e_\\lambda) / \\mathrm{Stab}_{\\mathrm{SO}(n)}(e_\\lambda)$ has order 1 or 2. A subgroup of finite index has the same dimension as the ambient group. Thus, $\\dim \\mathrm{Stab}_{\\mathrm{O}(n)}(e_\\lambda) = \\dim \\mathrm{Stab}_{\\mathrm{SO}(n)}(e_\\lambda)$ in all cases.\n        \\item Therefore, $\\dim(\\mathrm{O}(n) \\cdot e_\\lambda) = \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$.\n    \\end{itemize}\n    \\item So, $\\dim(G \\cdot e) = \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$.\n\\end{itemize}\nSubstituting these into the dimension formula for the fiber:\n$$ \\dim M_e = n(n+1) - (\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu). $$\n\n\\subsubsection*{3. Relevant Nilpotent Orbits}\n\nA pair of orbits $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ is \\emph{relevant} if the inequality $\\dim M_e \\leq \\frac{1}{2} \\dim M - \\frac{1}{2} (\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu)$ is an equality.\nSubstituting the formula for $\\dim M_e$ from Part 2, the condition for relevance becomes:\n$$ n(n+1) - (\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu) = \\frac{1}{2} n(n+1) - \\frac{1}{2} (\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu). $$\nThis simplifies to:\n$$ \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu = n(n+1). $$\nThe dimension of a nilpotent orbit is bounded by the dimension of the ambient Lie algebra:\n$$ \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\leq \\dim \\mathfrak{so}(n) = \\binom{n}{2} = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}. $$\n$$ \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\leq \\dim \\mathfrak{so}(n+1) = \\binom{n+1}{2} = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. $$\nSumming these two inequalities gives an upper bound:\n$$ \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\leq \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} + \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} = n^2. $$\nFor a pair of orbits to be relevant, we must have:\n$$ n(n+1) = \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\leq n^2. $$\nThe inequality $n(n+1) \\leq n^2$ implies $n^2+n \\leq n^2$, which means $n \\leq 0$.\nSince $n = \\dim W$ must be a non-negative integer, the only possibility is $n=0$.\n\nWe examine the case $n=0$:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item $W=0$, so $\\mathfrak{g}_1 = \\mathfrak{so}(0) = \\{0\\}$. The set $\\mathcal{P}^+_0$ contains only the empty partition $\\lambda=()$. This corresponds to the zero orbit $\\mathcal{O}_{()} = \\{0\\}$.\n    \\item $V=L$, so $\\mathfrak{g}_2 = \\mathfrak{so}(1) = \\{0\\}$. The set $\\mathcal{P}^+_1$ contains only the partition $\\mu=(1)$. This corresponds to the zero orbit $\\mathcal{O}_{(1)} = \\{0\\}$.\n\\end{itemize}\nThis pair must satisfy the existence condition from Part 1. The partition $\\lambda=()$ is obtained from $\\mu=(1)$ by decreasing its only part by 1. The part $\\mu_1=1$ is odd, so the condition is satisfied.\n\nFinally, we check the relevance condition for $n=0$:\n$$ \\dim \\mathcal{O}_{()} + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_{(1)} = 0(0+1). $$\nSince both orbits are the zero orbit, their dimensions are 0. The condition becomes $0+0=0$, which is true.\n\nTherefore, there is exactly one relevant nilpotent orbit pair, which occurs for $n=0$: the pair of zero orbits $(\\mathcal{O}_{()}, \\mathcal{O}_{(1)})$.\n\n\\section*{Verification Report}\n\n\\subsection*{Summary}\n\n\\textbf{Final Verdict:} The solution's approach is viable and the conclusion is correct, but it contains a Justification Gap in its interpretation of the problem statement.\n\n\\textbf{List of Findings:}\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Location:} \"The action of $G = \\mathrm{O}(n) \\times \\mathrm{SO}(n+1)$ on $X = \\mathrm{SO}(n+1)$ given by $(g_1, g_2) \\cdot g = g_1 g g_2^{-1}$ is transitive.\"\n    \\begin{itemize}\n        \\item \\textbf{Issue:} Justification Gap - The problem statement provides a formula for the group action, `$(g_1, g_2) \\cdot g = (g_1 g g_2^{-1}, g_2)$`, which is likely a typographical error as its codomain is not $X$. The solution uses the standard action `$(g_1, g_2) \\cdot g = g_1 g g_2^{-1}$ without acknowledging the discrepancy or justifying this choice. While this interpretation is mathematically sound and likely the intended one, a rigorous solution must address such ambiguities.\n    \\end{itemize}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection*{Detailed Verification Log}\n\n\\subsubsection*{Part 0: Preliminaries: The Embedding of $\\mathrm{O}(W)$ into $\\mathrm{SO}(V)$}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Quote:} \"With respect to an orthonormal basis compatible with the decomposition $V=W \\oplus L$, we define the embedding $\\rho: \\mathrm{O}(W) \\to \\mathrm{GL}(V)$ by:\n    $$ \\rho(g_1) = \\begin{pmatrix} g_1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\det(g_1) \\end{pmatrix}. $$\n    Since $g_1$ is an isometry of $W$ and $\\det(g_1) = \\pm 1$, $\\rho(g_1)$ is an isometry of $V$. Its determinant is $\\det(\\rho(g_1)) = \\det(g_1)^2 = 1$, so $\\rho(g_1) \\in \\mathrm{SO}(V)$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. The verification is straightforward. For $g_1 \\in \\mathrm{O}(W)$, we have $g_1^T g_1 = I$. Then $\\rho(g_1)^T \\rho(g_1) = \\mathrm{diag}(g_1^T g_1, (\\det g_1)^2) = \\mathrm{diag}(I, 1) = I$. So $\\rho(g_1) \\in \\mathrm{O}(V)$. The determinant is $\\det(g_1) \\cdot \\det(g_1) = 1$, so $\\rho(g_1) \\in \\mathrm{SO}(V)$. The map is an injective homomorphism, thus an embedding.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Quote:} \"The Lie algebra of $G_1 = \\mathrm{O}(W)$ is $\\mathfrak{g}_1 = \\mathfrak{so}(W)$. The induced Lie algebra homomorphism $d\\rho: \\mathfrak{so}(W) \\to \\mathfrak{so}(V)$ is the standard inclusion $A \\mapsto \\mathrm{diag}(A, 0)$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. The Lie algebra of $\\mathrm{O}(W)$ is indeed $\\mathfrak{so}(W)$. For $A \\in \\mathfrak{so}(W)$, we have $\\mathrm{tr}(A)=0$. The curve $\\rho(\\exp(tA)) = \\mathrm{diag}(\\exp(tA), \\det(\\exp(tA))) = \\mathrm{diag}(\\exp(tA), \\exp(t \\cdot \\mathrm{tr}(A))) = \\mathrm{diag}(\\exp(tA), 1) = \\exp(\\mathrm{diag}(tA, 0))$. The derivative at $t=0$ gives $d\\rho(A) = \\mathrm{diag}(A, 0)$. This justifies the problem's setup.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsubsection*{Part 1: Condition for $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\subseteq \\mu(M)$}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Quote:} \"The condition $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\subseteq \\mu(M)$ means that for any pair $(e_\\lambda, e_\\mu) \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$, there exists $(g, \\xi) \\in G_2 \\times \\mathfrak{g}_2$ such that $\\mu(g, \\xi) = (e_\\lambda, e_\\mu)$... Thus, for any $e_\\lambda \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$, there must exist some $\\xi' \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ such that $p(\\xi') = e_\\lambda$. This is equivalent to the condition $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\subseteq p(\\mathcal{O}_\\mu)$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. The reasoning is sound. From the moment map formula, $\\mu_2(g, \\xi) = -\\xi = e_\\mu$ implies $\\xi = -e_\\mu$. Since $\\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ is a cone, $-e_\\mu \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$. The first component gives $\\mu_1(g, -e_\\mu) = p(\\mathrm{Ad}(g)(-e_\\mu)) = e_\\lambda$. The set $\\{\\mathrm{Ad}(g)(-e_\\mu) \\mid g \\in G_2\\}$ is the orbit $\\mathcal{O}_\\mu$. Thus, for any $e_\\lambda \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$, there must be an element $\\xi' \\in \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ such that its projection $p(\\xi')$ is $e_\\lambda$. This is precisely the condition $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\subseteq p(\\mathcal{O}_\\mu)$.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Quote:} \"This theorem implies that the condition $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\subseteq p(\\mathcal{O}_\\mu)$ is equivalent to the condition on their closures, $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\subseteq \\overline{p(\\mathcal{O}_\\mu)}$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. The solution correctly identifies $(\\mathfrak{so}(n+1), \\mathfrak{so}(n))$ as a symmetric pair and applies a known, albeit advanced, theorem by T. Ohta. The use of this theorem is appropriate for the context.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Quote:} \"\\textbf{Theorem (Branching Rule for $\\mathfrak{so}_{n+1} \\downarrow \\mathfrak{so}_n$)}: A nilpotent orbit $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\subset \\mathfrak{so}(n)$ is contained in the closure of the projection of a nilpotent orbit $\\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\subset \\mathfrak{so}(n+1)$ if and only if the partition $\\lambda$ can be obtained from $\\mu$ by decreasing one of its parts by 1.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. This is a standard result from the theory of nilpotent orbits, and it is stated correctly.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Quote:} \"In summary, the condition is that $\\lambda \\in \\mathcal{P}^+_n$ is obtained from $\\mu \\in \\mathcal{P}^+_{n+1}$ by decreasing one of its odd parts $\\mu_i$ by 1. If $\\mu_i > 1$, the multiplicity of $\\mu_i-1$ in $\\mu$ must be odd.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. The analysis of the constraints imposed by the partition conditions ($\\lambda \\in \\mathcal{P}^+_n, \\mu \\in \\mathcal{P}^+_{n+1}$) on the branching rule is meticulous and accurate. The two cases (decreasing an even part vs. an odd part) are handled correctly, and the conclusions are valid.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsubsection*{Part 2: Formula for $\\dim M_e$}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Quote:} \"The action of $G = \\mathrm{O}(n) \\times \\mathrm{SO}(n+1)$ on $X = \\mathrm{SO}(n+1)$ given by $(g_1, g_2) \\cdot g = g_1 g g_2^{-1}$ is transitive.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Justification Gap. The problem statement defines the action as $(g_1, g_2) \\cdot g = (g_1 g g_2^{-1}, g_2)$. This formula appears to be a typographical error, as the result is an element of $X \\times G_2$, not $X$. The solution uses the standard action $(g_1, g_2) \\cdot g = \\rho(g_1) g g_2^{-1}$, which is a map $G \\times X \\to X$. This is a reasonable interpretation, but the discrepancy is not mentioned or justified. A rigorous solution should explicitly state the assumption that the problem's formula is a typo and that the standard action is being used. Assuming this interpretation, the action is indeed transitive, as the orbit of the identity element $I \\in \\mathrm{SO}(n+1)$ is $\\{\\rho(g_1)g_2^{-1} \\mid g_1 \\in \\mathrm{O}(n), g_2 \\in \\mathrm{SO}(n+1)\\} = \\rho(\\mathrm{O}(n))\\mathrm{SO}(n+1) = \\mathrm{SO}(n+1)$.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Quote:} \"...the dimension of the fiber is constant and given by the formula: $ \\dim \\mu^{-1}(e) = \\dim M - \\dim(G \\cdot e). $ \"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. This is a standard formula for the dimension of the fibers of the moment map for the cotangent lift of a transitive group action on a manifold. Its use here is appropriate.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Quote:} \"$\\dim(G \\cdot e) = \\dim(\\mathrm{O}(n) \\cdot e_\\lambda) + \\dim(\\mathrm{SO}(n+1) \\cdot e_\\mu)$... Therefore, $\\dim(\\mathrm{O}(n) \\cdot e_\\lambda) = \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. The coadjoint action of the product group $G$ on $\\mathfrak{g}^* = \\mathfrak{g}_1^* \\times \\mathfrak{g}_2^*$ is component-wise. The argument that the dimension of the $\\mathrm{O}(n)$-orbit of $e_\\lambda$ is the same as the dimension of its $\\mathrm{SO}(n)$-orbit ($\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda$) is sound, as $\\mathrm{SO}(n)$ is the identity component of $\\mathrm{O}(n)$ and has the same dimension.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Quote:} \"$ \\dim M_e = n(n+1) - (\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu). $\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. This formula follows directly from the previous correct steps: $\\dim M = 2 \\dim \\mathrm{SO}(n+1) = n(n+1)$ and $\\dim(G \\cdot e) = \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsubsection*{Part 3: Relevant Nilpotent Orbits}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Quote:} \"A pair of orbits $\\mathcal{O}_\\lambda \\times \\mathcal{O}_\\mu$ is \\emph{relevant} if the inequality $\\dim M_e \\leq \\frac{1}{2} \\dim M - \\frac{1}{2} (\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu)$ is an equality... This simplifies to: $ \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu = n(n+1). $\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. The algebraic simplification is correct. Substituting $\\dim M_e = n(n+1) - (\\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu)$ and $\\dim M = n(n+1)$ into the equality yields the stated result.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Quote:} \"Summing these two inequalities gives an upper bound: $ \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\leq \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} + \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} = n^2. $ For a pair of orbits to be relevant, we must have: $ n(n+1) = \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\lambda + \\dim \\mathcal{O}_\\mu \\leq n^2. $ The inequality $n(n+1) \\leq n^2$ implies $n^2+n \\leq n^2$, which means $n \\leq 0$.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. The bounds for the dimensions of nilpotent orbits are standard. The summation and the subsequent logical deduction that the relevance condition can only hold if $n \\leq 0$ are flawless.\n\n    \\item \\textbf{Quote:} \"Since $n = \\dim W$ must be a non-negative integer, the only possibility is $n=0$. We examine the case $n=0$: ... This pair must satisfy the existence condition from Part 1... Finally, we check the relevance condition for $n=0$: ... $0+0=0$, which is true.\"\n    \\item \\textbf{Assessment:} Correct. The analysis of the $n=0$ case is thorough and accurate. The solution correctly identifies the unique partitions $\\lambda=()$ and $\\mu=(1)$, verifies that they satisfy the existence condition derived in Part 1, and confirms that they satisfy the relevance condition. The conclusion that this is the only pair of relevant orbits is fully justified by the preceding argument.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\end{document}"
